# Bizniz

Bizniz turns natural-language problem statements into working,
tested applications — web stacks and native mobile apps — by pairing
**deterministic tooling** (provisioning, docker orchestration, hard
test/smoke/validation gates) with **LLM agents** that do the code
work under those gates.

It runs in two modes:

## Claude-native harness mode (primary)

Claude Code drives the loop directly, using bizniz as its toolkit:

- **`bizniz` CLI** — one gate surface for every project type:
  `projects`, `status`, `up/down`, `smoke`, `test`, `validate`,
  `perf`, `mcp`. Web services gate via compose/pytest/curl; Expo
  mobile workspaces gate via `tsc`/`jest`/`expo lint` and a release
  build → `adb install` → Maestro flow on an emulator. Every gate is
  a hard exit code.
- **`.claude/agents/`** — the pipeline's battle-tuned role prompts as
  subagents: `bizniz-coder` (Haiku), `bizniz-architect`,
  `bizniz-code-reviewer`, `bizniz-quality-engineer` (Opus, with the
  tests-only bias firewall).
- **`.claude/skills/`** — `/bizniz-fix` (gate-driven convergence:
  cluster failures by root cause → dispatch coders → diff-audit →
  re-gate until green or stalled) and `/bizniz-review` (parallel
  CR+QE + deterministic gates → one clustered defect report).
- **`.mcp.json`** — session-wide MCP server exposing cross-issue
  context (prior issues, test output, symbol validation, audit
  findings, auth contract) to any agent; tools take a project slug.

The working method: pin a contract, dispatch parallel subagents per
slice, diff-audit every result, verify every claim against the gates
independently, send diagnoses back to the same agent (it keeps its
context), and fix root causes in the harness/skeletons — not in the
generated project — whenever a bug is the factory's fault.

**First product shipped this way: [Muvnit](https://muvnit.com)** —
a trucking-paperwork app (Expo + FastAPI + FusionAuth + a tiered
document-AI worker). Four milestones, all gates green, extraction
proven on 100+ real receipts/BOLs: tier-1 tesseract heuristics, then
two-model convergence (local Ollama VLMs, OpenRouter-ready) where
fields are confirmed only when independent models agree — and
flagged for review with candidates when they don't.

## Autonomous pipeline mode (legacy/batch)

`examples/v2_build.py` runs the original agent pipeline end-to-end
without supervision: Planner → Architect → Provisioner (+ FusionAuth
agent) → per-service Engineer → Coder/Tester/Debugger loops →
QE + CR review/repair → integration tests (pytest+httpx, Playwright)
against the live stack → UX review → refactor. Same skeletons, same
gates, same artifacts. Still the right tool for hands-off batch
builds and A/B baselines (`bizniz/perf_log/` measures both modes).

## Skeletons

Projects are seeded from contract-bearing skeleton repos
(`bizniz-skeleton-fastapi`, `-react`, `-angular`, `-expo`, …). Each
ships a `SKELETON.md` hard contract: auto-discovery conventions
(FastAPI routes, expo-router screens), auth delegation to FusionAuth,
the gate table, and the accumulated field lessons (Hermes runtime
constraints, honest API typing, network-security config plugins).

## Orientation

Read `CLAUDE.md` for the current session state, commands, and
invariants. Recent narrative: `docs/changes/`.
